::U3L4Q01::Which statement best defines media literacy? {=The ability to access, analyze, evaluate, and create media messages ~Believing popular messages ~Avoiding all media ~Counting followers}

::U3L4Q02::What is a target audience? {=The group a message is designed to influence ~Everyone who owns a phone ~Only experts ~The product manufacturer}

::U3L4Q03::A personal success story is primarily what type of support? {=A testimonial ~Controlled research ~A systematic review ~A nutrition credential}

::U3L4Q04::Why can a filtered image not prove a health claim? {=Appearance does not establish health status or causation ~Filters always make claims false ~Images are never useful ~Only text can be evidence}

::U3L4Q05::What does a paid sponsorship create? {=A possible conflict of interest requiring closer evidence review ~Automatic proof of fraud ~A scientific qualification ~A control group}

::U3L4Q06::Which is the strongest evidence? {=Transparent research with appropriate methods and limitations ~A celebrity testimonial ~A large follower count ~An edited before-and-after image}

::U3L4Q07::A red-flag word such as â€œmiracleâ€ should lead a reader to do what? {=Investigate the evidence carefully ~Assume the opposite is true ~Share immediately ~Ignore the source}

::U3L4Q08::Which is a universal claim? {=This works for everyone ~Some people may respond differently ~Evidence is limited ~Consult a qualified professional}

::U3L4Q09::Why is â€œ7 of 10 volunteers felt betterâ€ limited evidence? {=The sample is small and methods, controls, and objective outcomes are missing ~Seven is always too many ~Feelings never matter ~Percentages cannot be evidence}

::U3L4Q10::What is social comparison? {=Evaluating oneself in relation to others ~Comparing two research methods ~Reading a label ~Checking a credential}

::U3L4Q11::Which feature is social proof rather than scientific evidence? {=Two million followers ~A disclosed research method ~A comparison group ~Replication}

::U3L4Q12::Which question best checks creator qualifications? {=What relevant education, license, or expertise is documented? ~How attractive is the creator? ~How many likes appeared? ~Is the video short?}

::U3L4Q13::What is missing information? {=Relevant detail needed to judge a claim that the message does not provide ~Any fact the reader dislikes ~The product logo ~The creator's first name}

::U3L4Q14::Which conclusion is appropriately limited? {=The claim is inadequately supported as presented ~The product can never work for anyone ~All influencers are dishonest ~Popularity proves effectiveness}

::U3L4Q15::What is an endorsement? {=Public support for a person, product, or claim ~A research control ~A nutrient value ~A privacy policy}

::U3L4Q16::What is a credibility check designed to evaluate? {=Whether the source and support deserve confidence ~Whether a message is entertaining ~Whether colors are attractive ~Whether it is brief}

::U3L4Q17::Which message shows responsible qualification? {=Results may vary, and current evidence has limitations ~Guaranteed transformation in ten days ~Works for everyone ~No questions needed}

::U3L4Q18::Why compare a promotional claim with a credible public-health source? {=To test the claim against qualified evidence and acknowledged limits ~To count which has more words ~To choose the brighter design ~To avoid all decisions}

::U3L4Q19::An algorithm can influence belief by doing what? {=Repeatedly exposing a user to similar messages ~Proving the messages scientifically ~Licensing creators ~Removing every advertisement}

::U3L4Q20::What is diet culture in this lesson's context? {=Messages that attach moral or social value to bodies, food, or restrictive eating ~Any discussion of nutrition ~A government label ~A research design}

::U3L4Q21::Which statement is a marketing claim? {=Our snack makes good people feel guilt-free ~The study used a comparison group ~The label lists 5 grams of fiber ~The author reports limitations}

::U3L4Q22::Which fact most clearly signals conflict of interest? {=The creator earns money when followers buy the plan ~The creator cites a source ~The message has captions ~The product has ingredients}

::U3L4Q23::What is misinformation? {=False or inaccurate information shared regardless of intent ~Only paid advertising ~Any unpopular opinion ~A disclosed limitation}

::U3L4Q24::What is disinformation? {=False information deliberately created or shared to deceive ~An accidental error ~A scientific limitation ~A qualified recommendation}

::U3L4Q25::Which response avoids body stigma? {=Evaluate the message and evidence without judging anyone's body ~Assume appearance shows discipline ~Rank bodies by health ~Require personal weight disclosure}

::U3L4Q26::Why is urgency a persuasive technique? {=It can push action before careful evaluation ~It creates a control group ~It proves expertise ~It reports limitations}

::U3L4Q27::Which detail is a testimonial? {=â€œI used it and felt amazingâ€ ~A randomized comparison found a measured difference ~The author lists methods ~The report discloses limitations}

::U3L4Q28::What should follow identification of a claim? {=Examine evidence, source qualifications, conflicts, and missing information ~Accept it if repeated ~Judge the creator's appearance ~Count emojis}

::U3L4Q29::Which message is most credible? {=A qualified source cites evidence, states limitations, and avoids guarantees ~A celebrity promises instant results ~A sponsored post gives only a filtered image ~An anonymous account says everyone agrees}

::U3L4Q30::Why are appearance and popularity weak substitutes for credibility? {=Neither demonstrates relevant expertise, sound methods, or causal evidence ~Both are always deceptive ~Credibility depends only on age ~Health claims need no evidence}

